1. Let x be the number of cookies Henry baked last year. 2. Henry wants to bake twice as many cookies as last year, so he intended to bake 2x cookies. 3. He baked 15 more cookies than intended, so he baked 2x + 15 cookies. 4. He dropped off 5 cookies for cooling, so he had 2x + 15 - 5 = 2x + 10 cookies. 5. He ended up with 110 cookies, so 2x + 10 = 110. 6. Subtract 10 from both sides of the equation: 2x = 100. 7. Divide both sides by 2: x = 50.
Therefore, Henry baked 50 cookies last year.

\boxed{50}

1. Determine the number of fruits each person bought.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Morisette: 5 apples + 8 oranges = 13 fruits
    \item Kael:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Apples: 2 * 5 apples = 10 apples
        \item Oranges: 8 oranges / 2 = 4 oranges
        \item Total for Kael: 10 apples + 4 oranges = 14 fruits
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
2. Calculate the total number of fruits.
Total fruits = Morisette's fruits + Kael's fruits = 13 + 14 = 27 fruits.

\boxed{27}

